<section xml:id="sec-mod9-study-guide">
  <title>Companion Reading and Study Guide</title>
  <p>Reading: Zorn, Section 4.4. Estimated time: 60 minutes.</p>

    <p>
      This guide accompanies Zorn's &#xa7;4.4 (<q>Sequences and Series of
      Functions</q>). Section 4.4 introduces pointwise and uniform convergence,
      proves the two main preservation theorems (continuity and integrability),
      and develops the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test for series of functions.
      Read the section before working the questions below.
    </p>
    <p>
      The theme running through all of &#xa7;4.4 is interchange of limits:
      when can we move <m>\lim_{n \to \infty}</m> past <m>\int</m>, past
      <m>\lim_{x \to a}</m>, past <m>\sum</m>? The answer in every case is:
      when the convergence is <em>uniform</em>. Keep this organizing question
      in mind as you read.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       POINTWISE AND UNIFORM CONVERGENCE
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod9-sg-ptwise">
    <title>Pointwise and Uniform Convergence (Zorn §4.4, opening definitions)</title>
    <p>
      Read Zorn's definitions of pointwise and uniform convergence carefully.
      The key structural difference is in the quantifier order.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-sg-1">
    <title>Writing Out the Quantifiers</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write both definitions with all quantifiers spelled out explicitly,
        using the format <m>\forall \cdots \exists \cdots \forall \cdots</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n \to f</m> pointwise on <m>S</m> (the <m>N</m> may
              depend on both <m>\varepsilon</m> and <m>x</m>).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly on <m>S</m> (the <m>N</m> depends
              only on <m>\varepsilon</m>).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Circle the one quantifier block that changes between (a) and (b).
              Explain in one sentence why this change makes uniform convergence
              stronger than pointwise convergence.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>f_n \to f</m> pointwise: <m>\forall x \in S,\ \forall \varepsilon
        \gt 0,\ \exists N(x, \varepsilon) \in \N,\ \forall n \geq N:
        |f_n(x) - f(x)| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly: <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0,\
        \exists N(\varepsilon) \in \N,\ \forall n \geq N,\ \forall x \in S:
        |f_n(x) - f(x)| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) The block <m>\exists N</m> moves before <m>\forall x</m> in the
        uniform case. This forces a single <m>N</m> to control the approximation
        simultaneously for every <m>x \in S</m>, rather than allowing <m>N</m>
        to grow without bound as <m>x</m> varies.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-sg-2">
    <title>The Sup-Norm Criterion</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Zorn states that <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly on <m>S</m> if and only
        if <m>\sup_{x \in S} |f_n(x) - f(x)| \to 0</m> as <m>n \to \infty</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              Explain in words why this criterion is equivalent to the
              definition of uniform convergence.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use the sup-norm criterion to verify that
              <m>f_n(x) = x/n</m> converges uniformly to <m>0</m> on
              <m>[0,1]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use the sup-norm criterion to show that <m>f_n(x) = x^n</m>
              does <em>not</em> converge uniformly to its pointwise limit on
              <m>[0,1]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) The sup over <m>x</m> is the worst-case error at stage <m>n</m>.
        Saying this sup <m>\to 0</m> means the worst-case error can be made
        smaller than any <m>\varepsilon</m>&#x2014;exactly what <q>one
        <m>N</m> works for all <m>x</m></q> means.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>\sup_{x \in [0,1]} |x/n - 0| = 1/n \to 0</m>. Uniform.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) The pointwise limit is <m>f(x) = 0</m> for <m>x \in [0,1)</m>
        and <m>f(1) = 1</m>. Then <m>\sup_{x \in [0,1]} |x^n - f(x)|
        \geq |x^n - 0|</m> for <m>x \in [0,1)</m>. As <m>x \to 1^-</m>,
        <m>x^n \to 1</m>, so the sup is at least <m>1</m> for every <m>n</m>.
        Hence the sup does not go to <m>0</m>; not uniform.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-sg-3">
    <title>Examples: Uniform or Not?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each sequence of functions, find the pointwise limit and determine
        (using the sup-norm criterion) whether convergence is uniform on the
        given set.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = \sin(x)/n</m> on <m>\R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = nx\,e^{-nx}</m> on <m>(0, \infty)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = \dfrac{1}{1 + nx}</m> on <m>[1, \infty)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = \dfrac{x^2 + nx}{n}</m> on <m>[0,1]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Pointwise limit <m>0</m>. <m>\sup_\R |\sin(x)/n| = 1/n \to 0</m>.
        Uniform on <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Pointwise limit <m>0</m> (for each fixed <m>x \gt 0</m>,
        <m>nx\,e^{-nx} \to 0</m>). But <m>\sup_{x \gt 0} nx\,e^{-nx} = 1/e</m>
        (max at <m>x = 1/n</m>), which does not go to <m>0</m>. <em>Not</em>
        uniform on <m>(0, \infty)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Pointwise limit <m>0</m>. On <m>[1,\infty)</m>:
        <m>1/(1+nx) \leq 1/(1+n) \to 0</m>, so
        <m>\sup_{x \geq 1} 1/(1+nx) = 1/(1+n) \to 0</m>. Uniform on
        <m>[1, \infty)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (d) <m>(x^2 + nx)/n = x^2/n + x \to x</m> pointwise. Sup-norm:
        <m>|x^2/n| \leq 1/n \to 0</m> on <m>[0,1]</m>. Uniform on
        <m>[0,1]</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       PRESERVATION THEOREMS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod9-sg-preserve">
    <title>Preservation of Continuity and Integrability</title>
    <p>
      Read Zorn's proofs that uniform convergence preserves continuity and
      allows interchange of limit and integral.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-sg-4">
    <title>Uniform Limit Preserves Continuity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              State the theorem: if <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly on <m>[a,b]</m>
              and each <m>f_n</m> is continuous, then <m>f</m> is continuous.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Outline the <m>3\varepsilon</m> proof strategy: for fixed
              <m>x_0</m> and given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, name the three
              terms that appear in the triangle inequality
              <m>|f(x) - f(x_0)| \leq \cdots</m> and identify which piece
              of information controls each term.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Why does the proof fail if convergence is only pointwise?
              Trace through the argument and identify the step that breaks.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Standard statement: uniform limit of continuous functions is
        continuous.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>|f(x) - f(x_0)| \leq |f(x) - f_N(x)| + |f_N(x) - f_N(x_0)|
        + |f_N(x_0) - f(x_0)|</m>.
        Term 1 and Term 3 are controlled by uniform convergence (choose
        <m>N</m> so that <m>\sup |f_n - f| \lt \varepsilon/3</m>).
        Term 2 is controlled by continuity of <m>f_N</m> (choose <m>\delta</m>
        so that <m>|x - x_0| \lt \delta \Rightarrow |f_N(x) - f_N(x_0)|
        \lt \varepsilon/3</m>).
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) With only pointwise convergence, the <m>N</m> chosen in Step 1
        depends on <m>x</m>&#x2014;a different <m>N</m> is needed near
        <m>x_0</m> and away from it. The step that breaks is the choice of
        a single <m>f_N</m> before <m>x</m> is chosen. Every <m>f_N</m> is
        continuous, so Term 2 is never the problem. The problem is Term 1:
        an <m>N</m> chosen so that <m>|f_N(x_0) - f(x_0)| \lt \varepsilon/3</m>
        need not give <m>|f_N(x) - f(x)| \lt \varepsilon/3</m> at the other
        points <m>x</m> near <m>x_0</m>, and if we enlarge <m>N</m> to suit
        a particular <m>x</m>, the <m>\delta</m> from continuity of
        <m>f_N</m> changes, so the set of points <m>x</m> under control
        changes too. No single <m>N</m> and <m>\delta</m> need work for every
        <m>x</m> with <m>|x - x_0| \lt \delta</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-sg-5">
    <title>Term-by-Term Integration</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              State the term-by-term integration theorem precisely.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Assume, in addition, that the limit <m>f</m> is Riemann
              integrable on <m>[a,b]</m>; you will prove this part of the
              theorem in <xref ref="ex-mod9-ps-c1"/>. Under this assumption,
              prove that <m>\int_a^b f = \lim_{n \to \infty} \int_a^b f_n</m>.
              <em>Hint:</em> estimate
              <m>\left|\int_a^b f_n - \int_a^b f\right| \leq
              \int_a^b |f_n - f| \leq (b-a) \cdot \sup_{x} |f_n(x) - f(x)|</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} \int_0^1 x^n\,dx</m> and
              compare to <m>\int_0^1 \lim_{n \to \infty} x^n\,dx</m>.
              Does this contradict the theorem? Why or why not?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) If <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly on <m>[a,b]</m> and each <m>f_n</m>
        is Riemann integrable, then <m>f</m> is integrable and
        <m>\int_a^b f = \lim_{n \to \infty} \int_a^b f_n</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Write <m>s_n = \sup_{x \in [a,b]} |f_n(x) - f(x)|</m>. By the
        sup-norm criterion, <m>s_n \to 0</m>; in particular <m>s_n</m> is
        finite for all large <m>n</m>. We assume, as the exercise allows,
        that <m>f</m> is integrable. For <m>n</m> large enough that
        <m>s_n</m> is finite, <m>f_n - f</m> is integrable (linearity), so
        <m>|f_n - f|</m> is integrable (<xref ref="ex-mod8-ps-s5"/>), and
        <m>|f_n - f| \leq s_n</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>. By monotonicity of the
        integral,
        <md>\left|\int_a^b f_n - \int_a^b f\right| = \left|\int_a^b
        (f_n - f)\right| \leq \int_a^b |f_n - f| \leq (b-a) \cdot s_n \to 0.</md>
        Hence <m>\int_a^b f = \lim_{n \to \infty} \int_a^b f_n</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) <m>\int_0^1 x^n\,dx = 1/(n+1) \to 0</m>. But
        <m>\int_0^1 \lim x^n\,dx = \int_0^1 f\,dx</m> where <m>f(x) = 0</m>
        on <m>[0,1)</m> and <m>f(1) = 1</m>: this integral also equals
        <m>0</m> (the single point <m>x=1</m> does not affect the integral).
        So the two agree&#x2014;no contradiction. But the convergence
        <m>x^n \to f</m> is <em>not</em> uniform, so the theorem does not
        apply; this is a case where the interchange happens to work despite
        non-uniform convergence.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod9-sg-interchange-fails">
    <title>When the Interchange Fails</title>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="ex-mod9-sg-5"/>(c) the convergence is not uniform, yet
      the limit and the integral can still be interchanged. Pointwise
      convergence does not always cause trouble, but it can. Here is a
      sequence for which the interchange fails. For <m>n \in \N</m> and
      <m>x \in [0,1]</m>, let
      <md>g_n(x) = 2n^2 x\,e^{-n^2x^2},</md>
      the second sequence in <xref ref="ex-mod9-sa-3"/>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>The pointwise limit is <m>0</m>.</em> First, <m>g_n(0) = 0</m> for
      every <m>n</m>. Now fix <m>x \in (0,1]</m> and put <m>u = n^2x^2</m>,
      so that <m>g_n(x) = (2/x)\,u\,e^{-u}</m>. By
      <xref ref="par-mod7-sg-43-taylor" text="custom">Taylor's Theorem</xref>
      applied to the Taylor polynomial of order <m>1</m> of <m>e^u</m> at
      <m>0</m>, <m>e^u = 1 + u + e^c u^2/2</m>
      for some <m>c</m> between <m>0</m> and <m>u</m>; since <m>e^c \geq 1</m>,
      this gives <m>e^u \geq u^2/2</m> for <m>u \gt 0</m>. Hence
      <m>0 \leq u\,e^{-u} \leq 2/u</m>, so
      <m>0 \leq g_n(x) \leq 4/(n^2x^3)</m>, which tends to <m>0</m> as
      <m>n \to \infty</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>The integrals tend to <m>1</m>.</em> Since
      <m>\frac{d}{dx}\left[-e^{-n^2x^2}\right] = g_n(x)</m>, FTC Part 2
      (<xref ref="ch-mod8" text="custom">Module 8</xref>) gives
      <md>\int_0^1 g_n(x)\,dx = 1 - e^{-n^2} \to 1.</md>
      But the pointwise limit is the zero function, whose integral is
      <m>0</m>. So
      <md>\lim_{n \to \infty} \int_0^1 g_n(x)\,dx = 1 \neq 0 = \int_0^1 \lim_{n \to \infty} g_n(x)\,dx.</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      The term-by-term integration theorem now tells you, with no further
      computation, that <m>g_n \to 0</m> is not uniform on <m>[0,1]</m>:
      if it were, the two numbers would agree. You can also see this
      directly: <m>g_n(1/n) = 2n/e</m>, so
      <m>\sup_{x \in [0,1]} |g_n(x) - 0| \geq 2n/e</m>, which does not
      tend to <m>0</m>. The graph of <m>g_n</m> is a spike near
      <m>x = 0</m> that grows taller and narrower while the area under it
      approaches <m>1</m>. Pointwise convergence cannot detect the spike,
      because the spike moves toward <m>0</m> and eventually passes every
      fixed <m>x \gt 0</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <!-- ============================================================
       WEIERSTRASS M-TEST AND POWER SERIES
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod9-sg-mtest">
    <title>The Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test and Power Series</title>
    <p>
      Read Zorn's statement and proof of the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test, and
      his treatment of power series as a special case.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-sg-6">
    <title>Applying the <m>M</m>-Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Apply the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test to each series. State the bound
        <m>M_n</m>, verify <m>\sum M_n \lt \infty</m>, and conclude.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{\cos(nx)}{n^2}</m>
              on <m>\R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{x^n}{n!}</m>
              on <m>[-R, R]</m> for any fixed <m>R \gt 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{x}{3}\right)^n</m>
              on <m>[-2, 2]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>M_n = 1/n^2</m>; <m>|\cos(nx)/n^2| \leq 1/n^2</m>;
        <m>\sum 1/n^2 \lt \infty</m>. Uniform and absolute convergence on
        <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>M_n = R^n/n!</m>; <m>|x^n/n!| \leq R^n/n!</m> for
        <m>x \in [-R,R]</m>; <m>\sum R^n/n! = e^R \lt \infty</m>.
        Uniform convergence on <m>[-R,R]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) <m>M_n = (2/3)^n</m>; <m>|(x/3)^n| \leq (2/3)^n</m> on
        <m>[-2,2]</m>; <m>\sum (2/3)^n = 3 \lt \infty</m>.
        Uniform convergence on <m>[-2,2]</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-sg-7">
    <title>Radius of Convergence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each power series, use the Ratio Test to find the radius of
        convergence <m>R</m>. Then state (without proof, citing the
        <m>M</m>-Test) that the series converges uniformly on
        <m>[-r, r]</m> for any <m>0 \lt r \lt R</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} n\,x^n</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n x^{2n+1}}{2n+1}</m>
              (this is the power series for <m>\arctan(x)</m>)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{x^n}{n \cdot 2^n}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Ratio <m>= (n+1)|x|^{n+1}/(n|x|^n) = (1 + 1/n)|x| \to |x|</m>.
        Converges for <m>|x| \lt 1</m>; <m>R = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) The general term <m>|x|^{2n+1}/(2n+1)</m>; ratio
        <m>\to x^2</m>. Converges for <m>|x| \lt 1</m>; <m>R = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Ratio <m>= |x|^{n+1}/((n+1) \cdot 2^{n+1}) \cdot n \cdot 2^n/|x|^n
        = (n/(n+1)) \cdot (|x|/2) \to |x|/2</m>. Converges for
        <m>|x|/2 \lt 1</m>, i.e., <m>|x| \lt 2</m>; <m>R = 2</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-sg-8">
    <title>Term-by-Term Integration of a Power Series</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The geometric series gives
        <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (-1)^n x^n = \frac{1}{1+x}</m>
        for <m>|x| \lt 1</m>, and the convergence is uniform on
        <m>[-r,r]</m> for any <m>r \lt 1</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              Integrate both sides from <m>0</m> to <m>x</m> (for
              <m>|x| \lt 1</m>) using the term-by-term integration theorem
              to obtain a power series for <m>\ln(1+x)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Verify that the result agrees with the Taylor series for
              <m>\ln(1+x)</m> at <m>a = 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              At <m>x = 1</m>, the power series becomes
              <m>1 - 1/2 + 1/3 - 1/4 + \cdots</m>. What does the
              alternating series test tell you about the convergence of
              this series (from Module 5)?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>\int_0^x \frac{1}{1+t}\,dt = \int_0^x \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
        (-1)^n t^n\,dt = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (-1)^n \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}
        = x - \frac{x^2}{2} + \frac{x^3}{3} - \cdots</m>.
        Since <m>\int_0^x \frac{dt}{1+t} = \ln(1+x)</m>, we get
        <m>\ln(1+x) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{n+1} x^n}{n}</m>
        for <m>|x| \lt 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Taylor series: <m>f(0)=0</m>, <m>f'(x)=1/(1+x)</m> so
        <m>f'(0)=1</m>, <m>f''(x)=-1/(1+x)^2</m> so <m>f''(0)=-1</m>,
        etc. The pattern gives <m>\ln(1+x) = x - x^2/2 + x^3/3 - \cdots</m>.
        Agrees.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) At <m>x=1</m>: <m>\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1)^{n+1}/n</m>.
        Terms decrease to 0, alternating; by the Alternating Series Test
        (Module 5), this converges. Its sum is <m>\ln 2</m>, but the
        formula from (a) does not give this directly: (a) holds only for
        <m>|x| \lt 1</m>, and what is needed is the value of the series at
        <m>x = 1</m> itself. Abel's theorem gives results of this kind in
        general; here a direct estimate suffices, the same one used in
        <xref ref="ex-mod9-we-4" text="custom">Worked Example 4</xref>. For
        <m>t \in [0,1]</m>, the finite geometric sum gives
        <md>\frac{1}{1+t} = \sum_{k=0}^{n} (-1)^k t^k + (-1)^{n+1}\frac{t^{n+1}}{1+t}.</md>
        Integrating from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m> (FTC Part 2),
        <md>\ln 2 - \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{(-1)^k}{k+1} = (-1)^{n+1}\int_0^1 \frac{t^{n+1}}{1+t}\,dt,</md>
        and since <m>0 \leq t^{n+1}/(1+t) \leq t^{n+1}</m> on <m>[0,1]</m>,
        monotonicity of the integral bounds the right side in absolute value
        by <m>\int_0^1 t^{n+1}\,dt = 1/(n+2) \to 0</m>. So the partial sums
        converge to <m>\ln 2</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

</section>
